ed the strategic consequences of blocking G11 versus K11. If I block at G11, White can still threaten L11, and keep the initiative, and forces a response. If I block K11, I shut down the threat, ending the line on the other side with my F11 stone, and White can not extend to create a 5-in-a-row.\n\nSo, the optimal move is K11. This blocks the potential winning open four for White and neutralizes the immediate threat. K11 creates a blocked 4. I have confirmed that K11 is free and on the board, by cross-checking all coordinates.\n\n**Here\'s the JSON output:**\n\n```json\n{"col": "K", "row": 11}\n```\n\n**Evaluation:**\n\nWhite presents a critical threat with a potential "open 4" on Row 11. However, playing K11 is the most effective defensive move. The placement neutralizes the direct threat, and it forces White to look elsewhere.\n\n**Reason:**\n\nBlack must block the white stone sequence on Row 11, specifically H11-I11-J11. Playing at K11 is better than G11 because it definitively shuts down the potential open four by creating a dead shape. If White plays at K11, Black blocks the White stones, and White cannot extend the line to make a win, while blocking at G11 merely delays the threat, and hands the initiative back to White to place a fourth stone, or play elsewhere.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board shows a critical situation on row 11. White has three consecutive stones at H11, I11, and J11. The spaces at G11 and K11 are currently empty. If White is allowed to play at K11, they will establish a continuous line of four stones (H11-K11) with open ends at both G11 and L11 (an "Open 4"). This would guarantee a win for White on the subsequent turn. Black has no immediate winning threats (such as a 4-in-a-row or Open 3) to counter-attack, so a defensive block is mandatory.\n\nReason: Black must block the threat on row 11. There are two potential blocking spots: G11 and K11.\n1. If Black blocks at G11, White can still play K11 to form a "blocked four" (H11-K11 blocked by G11). This would threaten a win at L11, forcing Black to respond again, allowing White to keep the initiative.\n2. If Black blocks at K11, White is prevented from forming the open four. Furthermore, White cannot win by playing G11 afterwards because Black already has a stone at F11, which would block the line on the other side (F11-G11-H11-I11-J11 is a blocked line).\nTherefore, playing at K11 is the most effective defensive move as it completely neutralizes the threat and stops White\'s momentum.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "K", "row": 11}'}
